## Technical Notes

- The JavaScript `%` operator returns the remainder of division, which matches standard modulo behavior for positive numbers
- No external libraries are required; this uses the native JavaScript modulo operator
- The implementation follows the existing patterns in the codebase for consistency
- TypeScript's exhaustive switch checking will ensure the new case is handled correctly

## Dependencies

No additional dependencies are required. The existing yargs library (version 18.0.0) already supports the `%` character as a valid choice option.

This PR adds `getTreeUsing` which registers a callback that developers can use to customize what the tree looks like instead of automatically mapping it to a relationship.

This allows users to pass in an array in the proper format such as:

```
[
    [
        'name' => 'Parent Name',
        'value' => 'parent_value',
        'children' => [
            [
                'name' => 'Child Name',
                'value' => 'child_value',
                'children' => [], // optional if no children
            ],
           // etc...
        ],
    ],
    // etc...
]
```

The resulting value is an array made of the values of all selected options.

At some point the intended functionality of the bar area was lost in `Split`  and it started being treated as the minimum bar thickness. This is despite the docs being quite clear:

```rust
/// The minimum splitter bar area defines the minimum thickness of the area
/// where pointer hit detection is done for the splitter bar.
/// The final hit detection area thickness is either this minimum
/// or the splitter bar thickness, whichever is greater.
///
/// This can be useful when you want to use a very narrow visual splitter bar,
/// but don't want to sacrifice user experience by making it hard to click on.
```

This PR restores that functionality. Only hit detection is done using the area. Child space calculation and bar painting are done based on the visual bar thickness.

I also renamed some of the local methods to make it more clear whether they operate on the area or not.

I also added a refinement to `bar_area_edges()` to always keep the defined bar area clickable. So when the bar gets shifted all the way to one side (which you want, to hide one child), you don't want half the accessbility bar area to get lost too. The new code will progressively shift the bar area to the side that has space for it.

